The Warning: end of file not at end of a line; newline inserted warning occurs because the GNU Assembler (gas) 
requires every assembly source file (typically .s or .S) to end with a blank newline character. 
If the final instruction or directive in your file sits on the very last line without a trailing line break, 
the compiler automatically inserts one to prevent syntax parsing issues and warns you about it. 

How to Fix ItOpen the source file or macro file generating the warning in your text editor.
Navigate to the very bottom of the file.Place your cursor at the end of the final line of 
code and press Enter to add a blank new line.Save the file and recompile. 
if you are generating assembly files programmatically via a script or command (like printf), 
ensure your string terminates with a \n newline character.
If you are using custom assembly macros, ensure that the macro definitions block closes 
properly and contains a final line break inside or immediately following the .endm directive

